{"kind":"assert","text":"C3 takes the two replica facts of C2 review `472e271a`.\n\n1. The hash of a row. `ScopeHandle.log` hashes the bytes of each row,\n   parses them strictly and checks the scope and the seq\n   (`packages/client/src/handle.ts`, the loop from line 177). So a\n   replica that reads through the handle folds no row with a wrong\n   hash. C3 states that dependence in its note and adds one case: a\n   page with one wrong hash folds nothing and closes no custody\n   record. C3 adds no second hash in the replica.\n2. A read that nothing awaits. The constructor and `#observed` call\n   `void this.read()`. A `TransportError` from the handle, or a\n   listener that throws in `#publish`, rejects that promise, and\n   nothing handles it. C3 repairs both:\n   - A listener that throws does not break the read. The replica\n     keeps its state and calls the next listener.\n   - A failed read that nothing awaits leaves no unhandled rejection.\n   - A page that fails the handle's checks is a protocol failure, and\n     the replica stops. A network failure is not, and the replica\n     stays `behind` and reads again at the next trigger.\n3. Before the source change, tell me whether `TransportError` tells a\n   protocol failure from a network failure, and which stop cause you\n   propose. A new cause changes the client note, so I rule on its\n   name first. The rest of C3 does not wait.\n\nOne named case covers each repair, and one control covers the stop.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}